<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!-- The app's component vocabulary (F-035). Before this file, App.xaml held ten brushes, nine
         converters, and exactly ONE keyed control style — so every button in the app was a
         hand-copied attribute set. 63 sites across 15 files repeat the same four themed attributes,
         which is why they drifted and why every previous fix had to be applied 63 times.

         WHAT THESE STYLES DO NOT REACH (review gate, 2026-08-05): two buttons are constructed in
         C# rather than markup — CaptionColorPickerWindow's palette swatches and SquadLaunchWindow's
         Remove — and they set their brushes via FindResource, not via a Style. They bind the same
         derived edge and are guarded by their own test, but a change made here does NOT reach them.
         Text inputs are a separate, deliberate gap: they keep the authored divider until stage 5.

         These styles own the THEMED attributes only: background, foreground, border. Padding,
         margin and font size stay at the call site, because those legitimately differ — a toolbar
         button and a dialog footer button are not the same size, and pretending otherwise would
         trade one bug for a layout sweep nobody asked for. -->

    <!-- Primary. Navy field, white text, cyan edge. Measured 9.39:1 in brand, 8.05:1 in midnight,
         4.90:1 in magenta-heat — this recipe was always fine. It is here so it stops being copied
         by hand, not because it was broken. -->
    <Style x:Key="PrimaryButtonStyle" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource CyanBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Secondary — the recipe F-025, F-030 and F-031 are all about.

         It differs from primary in exactly one attribute: the border. That single difference is the
         entire defect. Secondary bound DividerBrush, and Navy == Bg in all three built-in themes,
         so the fill contributed zero separation and the whole affordance rested on a border
         measuring 1.26:1 (brand), 1.16:1 (midnight), 1.14:1 (magenta-heat) against WCAG 1.4.11's
         3:1 floor. It shipped in the default theme at about a fifth of the required contrast.

         InteractiveEdgeBrush is derived by ContrastGuard from whatever the active theme supplies,
         so this clears 3:1 under any theme including ones users authored — without adding a slot
         they would have to fill in, and without touching DividerBrush, which is still correct
         everywhere it draws a separator rather than a control boundary.

         F-032: the label was MutedTextBrush, the same token helper prose uses. Muted-vs-White is
         2.42:1 in brand and 1.00:1 under flatline, so colour could not carry "quiet" under a theme
         this app supports — a control label and the paragraph beside it became the same thing.
         Weight carries it now, and the derived edge above already marks control-vs-prose. Note the
         label was never illegible: Muted on Navy measures 6.88:1 and passes AA. The defect was
         semantic, not contrast. -->
    <Style x:Key="SecondaryButtonStyle" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Normal" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>


    <!-- Secondary, strong label. The sweep that consolidated these found THREE recipes, not two.
         Since F-032 all three share a white label and differ by edge and weight: quiet secondary
         (derived edge, Normal), primary (cyan edge), and this one (derived edge, SemiBold).

         It gets the same edge fix and keeps its white label. Folding it into the quiet secondary
         would have fixed the contrast bug by demoting a dialog's main action to muted text, which
         is a legibility trade nobody asked for. Three recipes existed; three styles name them. -->
    <Style x:Key="SecondaryStrongButtonStyle" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ============================ INPUTS (F-029, F-049) ============================

         A text box IS a UI component under WCAG 1.4.11, exactly as much as a button is. Wave 5
         fixed buttons and left these on DividerBrush, which produced a visible inconsistency the
         close-out captured: under the flatline theme the Alerts panel shows a "Show" button with a
         clear outline sitting directly beside a webhook field with no boundary at all.

         Same derived brush, same reasoning. This is not new policy — it is the policy wave 5 wrote,
         applied to the controls it did not reach. What stays on DividerBrush is decoration: card
         edges, row rules, the popup edge on a context menu. Those are separators, and 1.4.11 does
         not govern separators. The fence test enforces that split by role. -->

    <!-- NO Background setter, and that is deliberate. Two fills are in use and both are right:
         RowBgBrush where the field sits on a card (Export, Import, Games) and NavyBrush where it
         sits on window chrome (Preferences). A field takes its fill from the surface behind it, so
         forcing one here would flatten a real distinction — the same reason the button styles leave
         padding and font size at the call site. The border is what drifted; the border is what this
         owns. -->
    <Style x:Key="AppTextBoxStyle" TargetType="TextBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type TextBox}}">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- A password field's boundary matters more than most, not less: there is no text to infer the
         control's extent from, so the outline is the only thing saying where to type. -->
    <Style x:Key="AppPasswordBoxStyle" TargetType="PasswordBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type PasswordBox}}">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- IMPLICIT, deliberately, and the only implicit style in this file (F-049). The alerts block
         was added twenty lines below an identical ComboBox and picked up none of its theming,
         because nothing made it inherit. Keying this would preserve exactly that failure mode: the
         next ComboBox someone adds would again theme itself by hand or not at all. Buttons and text
         boxes stay keyed — they have more than one legitimate recipe, so an implicit default there
         would silently overrule deliberate choices. A ComboBox has one. -->
    <Style TargetType="ComboBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type ComboBox}}">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource NavyBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource InteractiveEdgeBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ======================= SURFACES + HEADINGS (F-047, F-064) =======================

         WHAT THESE DO AND DO NOT FIX. They consolidate. Nine section cards in Preferences repeat
         the same four attributes by hand; Diagnostics builds its section heading in C# with a third
         spec no markup review would ever see. One style each, so the next change is one edit.

         They do NOT fix C5. A card is a fill and a radius, and under a theme that sets RowBg == Bg
         the grouping disappears entirely — no brush in this file can rescue that, because the
         problem is that grouping is carried by colour alone. The real fix is structural: a heading
         level above the card so the group reads from typography and rhythm rather than a fill. That
         is F-026, tagged [new-mechanism] in the register, and it is deliberately not smuggled in
         here. What this does is make F-026 a one-place edit instead of a nine-place one. -->

    <Style x:Key="CardBorderStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource RowBgBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="8" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="14" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,10" />
    </Style>

    <!-- C8's stated convention, finally expressed once: 13px SemiBold on the primary text token.
         Diagnostics had it at 12px on CyanBrush — accent colour doing structural work, which is the
         same mistake as grouping-by-fill and fails for the same reader. The generous top margin is
         the point: with the accent gone, separation between groups has to come from rhythm, not
         hue. -->
    <Style x:Key="SectionHeadingStyle" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource WhiteBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,18,0,6" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
